      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Eastern European factories often require less intensive supervision because the workforce is generally more accustomed to rigid industrial hierarchies and strict adherence to schedules, whereas emerging markets in SE Asia and Sub-Saharan Africa frequently demand a higher ratio of managers to workers to handle cultural differences and lower baseline discipline.\nFrom a logistics standpoint, the infrastructure in Eastern Europe is typically more reliable and standardized, reducing the need for constant oversight on shipping and quality control compared to the fragmented supply chains often found in developing regions.\nConsequently, budgeting for Eastern Europe usually involves lower costs for local management and training, while shifting production to SE Asia or Africa often necessitates significantly higher expenditures on personnel to maintain operational consistency and mitigate the risk of labor turnover.",
